ver]:decoration-primary [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:decoration-primary/30 [&amp;_a:not(.not-prose)]:underline-offset-2 [&amp;_h2:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h3:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;_h4:not(.not-prose):first-child]:mt-0"><h1 dir="auto" class="text-3xl">Behavioral Capture and Entropic Authoritarianism: An RSVP Interpretation and Critique of Jacob Ward</h1>
<h2 dir="auto" class="text-2xl mt-[1.5em]">Abstract</h2>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Jacob Ward's analysis of artificial intelligence (AI) as an amplifier of behavioral science, leading to a "world without choices," identifies critical risks in predictive governance and corporate centralization. This essay interprets Ward's critique through the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP) formalism, a field-theoretic model of collective cognition. RSVP reframes Ward's concerns as thermodynamic distortions: entropy collapse in the scalar field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span></span></span></span> and monopolar polarization in the vector agency field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span>. The discussion integrates formal definitions, lemmas, anticipated critiques, and design invariants for coherence-positive AI. A rigorous RSVP-based critique of Ward highlights ontological limitations in his model, emphasizing phase fragmentation over centralized control. Comparative analysis positions Ward within intellectual traditions on technology and power. The synthesis concludes that entropic authoritarianism represents a civilizational phase transition, necessitating field-theoretic re-engineering beyond legal remedies.</p>
<h2 dir="auto" class="text-2xl mt-[1.5em]">Introduction</h2>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Jacob Ward's examination of AI's intersection with behavioral science posits a profound erosion of human autonomy, culminating in predictive systems that automate decision-making and eliminate genuine choice. This perspective, while sociologically astute, acquires greater precision when mapped onto the RSVP formalism. In RSVP, cognitive and social systems emerge from the coupled dynamics of a scalar entropy field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, which encodes semantic openness and adaptive potential, and a vector agency field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 