<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Annotated Bibliography</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Arendt, H. (1951). The Origins of Totalitarianism. Schocken.
Arendt‚Äôs account of totalitarian governance elucidates how authoritarian power emerges not solely from centralized ideology, but from the breakdown of shared reality, erosion of pluralistic meaning, and the production of isolation. Within the RSVP framework, Arendt‚Äôs diagnosis corresponds to global scalar flattening (loss of shared meaning gradients) and vector disconnection (inhibition of reciprocal agency flows). Her work provides a foundational historical analogue for understanding entropic governance regimes that rely on meaning collapse rather than doctrinal coherence.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Balkin, J. (2018). ‚ÄúFree Speech in the Algorithmic Society.‚Äù UC Davis Law Review.
Balkin examines how algorithmic platforms restructure public discourse, displacing individual agency with infrastructural control. This complements the RSVP claim that semantic autonomy depends on distributed vector circulation rather than centralized broadcast. His legal framing supports the argument that restoring healthy agency fields requires boundary constraints that protect gradient formation and prevent monopolar capture of communicative flow.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Barab√°si, A.-L. (2016). Network Science. Cambridge University Press.
Barab√°si formalizes network dynamics, scaling laws, and centrality conditions that predict when hubs dominate system behavior. RSVP interprets such hub dominance as the emergence of vector field monopoles, destabilizing worldwide agency circulation. His account of preferential attachment offers a structural explanation for how corporate AI consolidates epistemic gravity and suppresses distributed semantic gradients.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Bateson, G. (1972). Steps to an Ecology of Mind. University of Chicago Press.
Bateson‚Äôs systems-theoretic view of cognition and communication underpins the RSVP assumption that meaning is relational and ecological rather than atomistic. His work anticipates RSVP‚Äôs claim that cognitive autonomy is a field phenomenon, not a property of isolated agents. The concept of feedback loops in communication maps directly to the circulation of vector fields of agency.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Bianconi, G. (2021). Higher-Order Networks. Cambridge University Press.
Bianconi‚Äôs formalization of multi-node interactions supports RSVP‚Äôs rejection of pairwise communication models. Behavioral capture in AI systems operates via higher-order influence simplices, not linear stimulus-response, aligning with her argument that network topology must account for group-level coupling. This work mathematically grounds RSVP‚Äôs emphasis on non-local semantic geometry and collective entropy dynamics.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Bridle, J. (2018). New Dark Age: Technology and the End of the Future. Verso.
Bridle argues that computational complexity generates systemic opacity rather than clarity, producing widespread epistemic paralysis. This aligns precisely with RSVP‚Äôs concept of entropic authoritarianism, where noise serves governance by collapsing global coherence while preserving local confusion. His thesis supports the claim that opacity is not a side effect but a tool of systemic entropy maximization.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Cialdini, R. (2007). Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion. Harper Business.
Cialdini‚Äôs taxonomy of persuasion mechanisms supplies empirical grounding for RSVP‚Äôs claim that AI-enabled messaging induces negative scalar curvature wells‚Äîaffective attractors that trap cognitive trajectories. His work demonstrates that persuasion exploits predictability, which RSVP reframes thermodynamically as engineered reductions in entropy gradients of choice.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Clark, A. (2015). Surfing Uncertainty. Oxford University Press.
Clark‚Äôs predictive processing model supports RSVP‚Äôs view that cognition is fundamentally gradient-based inference rather than symbolic reasoning. However, RSVP departs from Clark by emphasizing that prediction minimization alone can lead to agentic collapse if not balanced by entropy preservation; this tension directly informs the critique of AI optimization regimes.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Ellul, J. (1965). Propaganda: The Formation of Men‚Äôs Attitudes. Vintage.
Ellul identifies propaganda as an ecological system that reshapes cognition rather than persuading through argument. RSVP extends this by formalizing propaganda as vector field steering applied to emotionally compressed regions of semantic space. Ellul‚Äôs insight that propaganda saturates environments rather than messages anticipates the RSVP model of ambient cognitive governance.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Friston, K. (2010). ‚ÄúThe Free-Energy Principle.‚Äù Nature Reviews Neuroscience.
Friston‚Äôs free-energy framework formalizes cognition as uncertainty minimization. RSVP incorporates this insight but critiques it: when deployed at civilizational scale without entropy-preserving constraints, free-energy minimization drives semantic collapse rather than self-organization. This work is a crucial interlocutor for comparing prediction-minimization regimes vs. entropy-sustaining regimes.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Jaynes, E.T. (1957). ‚ÄúInformation Theory and Statistical Mechanics.‚Äù Physical Review.
