<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\title</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Behavioral Capture and Entropic Authoritarianism: An RSVP Interpretation and Critique of Jacob Ward}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\author</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\date</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{November 07, 2025}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">document</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\maketitle</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">abstract</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Jacob Ward's analysis of artificial intelligence (AI) as an amplifier of behavioral science, leading to a ``world without choices,'' identifies critical risks in predictive governance and corporate centralization </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\parencite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{ward2022loop}. This essay interprets Ward's critique through the Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum (RSVP) formalism, a field-theoretic model of collective cognition. RSVP reframes Ward's concerns as thermodynamic distortions: entropy collapse in the scalar field </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> and monopolar polarization in the vector agency field </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{v}</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">. The discussion integrates formal definitions, lemmas, anticipated critiques, and design invariants for coherence-positive AI. A rigorous RSVP-based critique of Ward highlights ontological limitations in his model, emphasizing phase fragmentation over centralized control. Comparative analysis positions Ward within intellectual traditions on technology and power, with particular expansion on phase transition regimes and a detailed comparison to Shoshana Zuboff's surveillance capitalism </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\parencite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{zuboff2019surveillance}. Mathematical proofs are enhanced with concrete examples. The synthesis concludes that entropic authoritarianism represents a civilizational phase transition, necessitating field-theoretic re-engineering beyond legal remedies.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">abstract</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Introduction}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Jacob Ward's examination of AI's intersection with behavioral science posits a profound erosion of human autonomy, culminating in predictive systems that automate decision-making and eliminate genuine choice </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\parencite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{ward2022loop}. This perspective, while sociologically astute, acquires greater precision when mapped onto the RSVP formalism. In RSVP, cognitive and social systems emerge from the coupled dynamics of a scalar entropy field </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, which encodes semantic openness and adaptive potential, and a vector agency field </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{v}(x,t)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, which encodes directed intent and communicative flow </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\parencite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{jaynes1957information,shannon1948mathematical}.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Autonomy persists under conditions of non-vanishing entropy gradients and divergence-free vector circulation:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">equation</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \neq</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">, \quad </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\approx</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">equation</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward's ``world without choices'' manifests as a degenerate fixed point:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">equation</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \rightarrow</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">, \quad \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\rightarrow</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v}_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \hat{u},</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">equation</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">where scalar gradients vanish and vector flow becomes unidirectional, devoid of reciprocal feedback. This essay merges Ward's insights with RSVP's mathematical structure, addresses potential critiques, proposes engineering invariants for countering collapse, critiques Ward's framework from an RSVP viewpoint, expands on phase transition regimes, compares to Zuboff's surveillance capitalism </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\parencite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{zuboff2019surveillance}, and situates his contributions intellectually.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
