<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{abstract}
Jacob Ward‚Äôs thesis that AI-driven behavioral science increasingly constructs a ‚Äúworld without choices‚Äù
identifies a genuine transformation of governance, agency, and social autonomy. However, Ward‚Äôs model
implicitly assumes centralized persuasion and hierarchical capture. In this manuscript, we demonstrate
that this assumption mischaracterizes the dominant 21st-century failure mode. Using the
\emph{Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum} (RSVP) formalism, we show that modern behavioral capture is not
producing singular authoritarian control, but \emph{entropic authoritarianism}‚Äîa distributed collapse of
semantic gradients driven by algorithmic microsurveillance, fragmented influence attractors, and the
erosion of coherent agency.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">We prove key results including: (1) a closed-form capture threshold <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Œ≤</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_c</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05278em;">Œ≤</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0528em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">c</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">‚Äã</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, (2) the equivalence between
predictive optimization and gradient collapse, (3) the instability of monopolar attention fields,
and (4) the condition under which agency turbulence becomes more dangerous than agency domination.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This work integrates theoretical foundations from thermodynamics, information theory, predictive
processing, network dynamics, and political theory to provide a mathematically grounded critique of
behavioral AI as a phase transition in collective cognition rather than a traditional power consolidation.
We conclude that the dominant risk mode is the topological death of meaning, not the centralization of it.
\end{abstract}</p>
