<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">% ===========================================================
\chapter{Introduction: The End of Choice Mischaracterized}
% ===========================================================</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Jacob Ward's central argument is that AI, fused with behavioral science, is rapidly automating decision trajectories,
reducing autonomy, and reshaping governance into a predictive system rather than a deliberative one. He describes
"the loop": a self-reinforcing cycle in which user behavior becomes both the target and training data for systems
that optimize prediction fidelity rather than human agency.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Ward is correct in identifying the \emph{where} (behavioral routing systems), the \emph{tool} (AI inference),
and the \emph{effect} (collapse of autonomous choice). But Ward‚Äôs account misidentifies the \emph{phase}.
He assumes the attractor is centralized control.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In reality, the 21st-century attractor is not a dictator‚Äîit is a \emph{thermal bath}. The coming regime
is not Orwellian precision but \emph{high-entropy stochastic governance} in which influence is diffuse,
personalization is absolute, and coherence is impossible. The risk is not that one agent captures the loop.
It is that the loop loses all consistent shape.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">We formalize this with the Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP), a field-theoretic model in which:</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{itemize}
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\item <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> is a scalar field representing semantic possibility gradients
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\item <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> is a vector field representing agency flow
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\item <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Œ≤</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05278em;">Œ≤</span></span></span></span> is the optimization pressure toward predictability
\end{itemize}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Ward sees collapsing freedom. RSVP sees collapsing \emph{derivatives of freedom}.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The collapse is not political. It is geometric.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">% ===========================================================
\chapter{RSVP Field Theory of Social Cognition}
% ===========================================================</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\section{Core Fields and Their Interpretation}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{definition}[Semantic Possibility Field]
