<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\chapter</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Introduction: The Geometry of Lost Choice}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Jacob Ward‚Äôs thesis is direct, unsettling, and timely: that the merger of behavioral science and artificial intelligence is ushering in a regime where autonomy is simulated rather than exercised, where prediction replaces agency, and where choice is an engineered illusion. Ward frames this as a historical rupture enabled by machine learning at scale, but the deeper structure of the problem lies beneath sociology, beneath market dynamics, and beneath even persuasion itself. It lies in the geometry of collective cognition.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Modern critiques of algorithmic power typically unfold along three trajectories:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">The economic critique</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, wherein AI extracts value and predicts behavior for profit (Zuboff).</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">The governance critique</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, wherein automated decision systems concentrate power (O‚ÄôNeil, Noble).</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">The epistemic critique</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, wherein reality becomes polluted by synthetic confusion (Bridle, Postman).</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward participates in all three, but goes further. His claim is not merely that people are targeted, governed, or confused. His claim is that the space of possible decisions itself is collapsing.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This paper argues that this collapse is not metaphorical. It is dynamical. It can be written in equations. It can be classified by phase. And most importantly, it is reversible only by engineering new field geometries of meaning and agency.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{From Persuasion to Field Collapse}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Classical persuasion targets beliefs. Algorithmic persuasion targets sequences of behavior. But field-theoretic persuasion‚Äîwhat modern AI enables‚Äîtargets the underlying state space on which beliefs and behaviors form. Rather than changing what a person thinks, it changes which thoughts are reachable at all.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In thermodynamic terms, autonomy requires gradients. A choice without gradient is not a decision but a slide into the nearest local minimum. Let semantic freedom be described by a scalar field:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t): \text{semantic possibility density at point } x \text{ and time } t</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward‚Äôs ‚Äúworld without choices‚Äù is the limiting case:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \to </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Agency is not merely possibility but directed motion through possibility. We represent this as a vector field:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{v}(x,t): \text{flow of intentional action through semantic state space}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Healthy cognition and healthy societies require circulation, not compression:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\approx</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward‚Äôs fear is realized when:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\gg</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">which indicates agency concentrated at source nodes and broadcast outward without return‚Äîwhat this paper will define as </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textit</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">vector monopolarity</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Why Ward‚Äôs Model Needs Formal Reconstruction}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward‚Äôs analysis excels at diagnosis but underspecifies mechanism. It does not distinguish between:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Totalitarian ordering</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: a single dominant attractor</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Entropy storm</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: fragmented, incoherent attractors that prevent stable meaning altogether</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Monopolar capture</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: centralization of agency vectors</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Semantic flatlining</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: collapse of meaning gradients independent of who controls them</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">itemize</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">These are not the same disease. They are different dynamical regimes. And they require different interventions.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">RSVP provides the missing formal layer: a field theory of cognition and civilization in which these conditions are not poetic analogies but categories of instability with mathematical signatures.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% ===========================================</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% RELATED WORK SECTION</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% ===========================================</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Related Work}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Concerns over the impact of algorithmically mediated choice arise from multiple intellectual traditions. The political economy of persuasive platforms has been extensively analyzed under the framing of </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">filter bubbles</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} and </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">echo chambers</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}, where recommenders reduce epistemic diversity and amplify selective exposure </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{pariser2011filter,nguyen2020echo,chaney2018filterBubble,huszar2022algorithmic}. These critiques diagnose a narrowing of discourse but do not formalize how possibility itself becomes dynamically unavailable, nor do they model the collapse of agency as a systemic phase transition.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Causal and information--geometric approaches have provided machinery for reasoning about representations without assuming a fixed coordinate system </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{peters2017elements,amari2016infoGeometry}. These tools underpin basis-agnostic notions of divergence, intervention, and semantic distance, yet are not typically applied to the dynamics of collective cognition or to societal-scale autonomy loss. In parallel, work on attention topology and diffusion over interaction networks demonstrates that online agency is highly structured, path-dependent, and prone to bottlenecking </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{zhu2021attention,verma2023agency,barabasi1999scale}. These results support the modeling of agency as a non-conservative flow field, but they do not characterize the consequences of extreme divergence or capture of that flow.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">A growing body of literature identifies structural pathologies in optimization-driven environments, including representational collapse, proxy overfitting, and gradient leakage under adversarial pressure </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{geiping2022gradient,grunwald2022collapse,bak2012selforganized}. This work provides empirical grounding for the claim that sufficiently strong optimization pressure can erase internal degrees of freedom. However, these studies focus primarily on model dynamics rather than the collapse of </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">cognitive possibility</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} in populations interacting with such models.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ethical critiques of recommender systems further highlight the incompatibility between engagement-maximizing objectives and epistemic health </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{dwork2020fairness,noble2018algorithms,pennycook2021misinfo}. While these contributions motivate design corrections centered on fairness, welfare, or misinformation mitigation, they lack a unifying mathematical description of autonomy loss as a collapse of traversable meaning-space. Moreover, formal limits on unsupervised decomposition and clustering </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{kleinberg2015impossibility} imply that any attempt to measure or discretize semantic space imposes non-neutral topology, reinforcing the need for observer-invariant formulations.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In contrast to these literatures, the </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} (RSVP) framework offers: (i) a dynamical theory of autonomy in which meaning and agency are fields, not attributes; (ii) a phase transition model of capture indexed by a critical ratio between semantic diffusion and optimization pressure; (iii) a distinction between centralized control and entropic authoritarianism, formalizing the latter as multi-point agency divergence without coherence; (iv) a prescriptive inversion for restorative systems, optimizing preserved cognitive phase volume rather than predictive compression; and (v) a basis-agnostic measurement proposal grounded in interventional semantic distance, avoiding coordinate-dependent embeddings.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">By unifying these elements, RSVP reframes algorithmic influence not as persuasion within a fixed space of meanings, but as a regeometry of the space itself. The consequence is a shift from normative arguments about user welfare to structural arguments about </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\emph</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">the conservation and dissipation of possible minds</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} under external optimization pressure.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% ===========================================</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% CHAPTER 2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(106, 153, 85);">% ===========================================</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\chapter</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{RSVP Field Foundations: Meaning, Agency, and Collective Dynamics}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The Relativistic Scalar‚ÄìVector Plenum (RSVP) treats cognition, discourse, and social coordination as </span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\textit</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230); font-style: italic;">field phenomena</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: continuous, interactive, and globally constrained rather than discrete, individual, or symbolic. This is not metaphor. It is a modeling stance grounded in thermodynamics, information theory, dynamical systems, and network topology. </span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Classical theories describe persuasion as signal injection and politics as agent competition. RSVP reformulates both as curvature problems in a shared manifold of semantic possibility.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Intellectual Lineage: From Entropy to Meaning}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">RSVP inherits and recombines five major historical streams:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Information theoretic entropy (Shannon, Jaynes)</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} ‚Äî meaning as a statistical distribution over alternative messages or states </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{shannon1948mathematical,jaynes1957information}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Non-equilibrium thermodynamics (Prigogine, Schr√∂dinger)</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} ‚Äî systems that remain coherent must export entropy and sustain gradients </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{prigogine1984order,schrodinger1944life}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Cybernetic feedback and distributed cognition (Bateson, Clark)</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} ‚Äî cognition is not bounded by skulls but flows through environments and agents </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{bateson1972ecology,clark2015surfing}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Biological autopoiesis (Maturana, Varela)</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} ‚Äî living systems are self-producing networks that maintain their organization through recursive operations </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{maturana1980autopoiesis}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Social autopoiesis (Luhmann)</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">} ‚Äî meaning systems are self-reproducing operations that maintain their own boundary through recursive communication </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{luhmann1995social}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
