<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">When optimization systems (e.g., recommender engines) minimize surprise, they introduce a field forcing:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\propto</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathcal{F}_{\text{predictive}}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">which drives semantic gradients toward zero ‚Äî precisely the ‚Äúworld without choices‚Äù Ward warns about.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Deep Integration with Friston's Free Energy Principle}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Friston's Free Energy Principle (FEP) posits that adaptive systems minimize variational free energy </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathcal{F}</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> to maintain low surprise relative to their internal model </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{friston2010freeenergy}. For a system with internal states </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\mu</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> and sensory observations </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">s</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathcal{F}[</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\mu</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">,s] = \mathbb{E}_q[\ln q(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">) - \ln p(s,</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">)] = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">)||p(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|s)) + \ln p(s)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">where </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">q(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> is the recognition density approximating the true posterior </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">p(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|s)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In RSVP, we identify:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t) </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\leftrightarrow</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \ln p(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|s) \quad \text{(local log-probability of meaningful states)} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);"> &amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\leftrightarrow</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \text{surprise gradient} \quad \text{(direction of prediction error)} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\leftrightarrow</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \text{active inference flow} \quad \text{(policy selection to reduce } \mathcal{F}\text{)}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The FEP dynamics become RSVP field equations under population scaling:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">= -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\kappa</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathcal{F}}{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} + \text{diffusion} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{\mathbf{v}} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">= -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\eta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathcal{F}}{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v}} + \text{gradient flow}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[FEP-Induced Collapse]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Collective application of the Free Energy Principle to a population of agents with shared predictive models necessarily collapses semantic gradients.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Consider </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">N</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> agents with shared recognition density </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">q(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">)</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">. The population free energy is:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathcal{F}_N = \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}{N} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\sum</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_i \mathcal{F}_i = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q||p) + \mathbb{E}_q[\ln p(s)]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Minimization drives </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">q \to p</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, reducing variance in </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\psi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">. In RSVP, this maps to:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} = -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\kappa</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathcal{F}_N}{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} = -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\kappa</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> (\ln q - \ln p) </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\leq</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">with equality only at the global minimum. Thus:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\frac{d}{dt} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\int</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> |</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \,</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> d</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\mu</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \leq</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\alpha</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \int</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> |</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \,</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> d</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\mu</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &lt; </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">for </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\alpha</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, proving exponential decay of semantic gradients.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The FEP thus provides the microfoundation for RSVP: individual agents reduce local surprise, but when their policies are coordinated by shared predictive models, the collective effect is global gradient collapse.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Maturana's Biological Autopoiesis and RSVP}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Maturana and Varela define autopoiesis as the self-maintenance of a network of processes that continuously regenerate the network that produces them </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{maturana1980autopoiesis}. The minimal autopoietic unit is a system that:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">1. Produces its own components</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">2. Constitutes a unity in space</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">3. Maintains its organization through recursive operations</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">In RSVP, we formalize this as:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">definition</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Autopoietic Cognitive System]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">A cognitive system is autopoietic if its meaning-production processes satisfy:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Self-production</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} = f(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">, \mathbf{v})</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> with </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">f</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> depending only on internal fields</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Boundary maintenance</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\oint</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\partial</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathcal{M}} \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> d\mathbf{A} = </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);"> \textbf</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214); font-weight: bold;">Recursive distinction</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}: </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> everywhere in the interior</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">definition</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Capture Violates Autopoiesis]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Behavioral capture necessarily violates autopoietic organization.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Platform algorithms inject external agency flow across the system boundary:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\oint</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v}_{\text{ext}} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> d\mathbf{A} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\neq</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">violating boundary maintenance. Simultaneously, optimization pressure </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> drives:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} = D_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> - </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &lt; </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">for </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, reducing </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> below </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> and eliminating recursive distinction capacity.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The restoration operators thus serve as autopoietic repair mechanisms:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">- Divergence restores distinction capacity</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">- Circulation enforces closure</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">- Re-entropization prevents operational stagnation</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Luhmann's Social Autopoiesis and RSVP}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Luhmann extends autopoiesis to social systems, defining them as networks of communication that reproduce themselves through recursive operations of distinction </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{luhmann1995social}. In RSVP terms:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}[Social Autopoietic Stability]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">A social meaning system maintains Luhmannian autopoiesis if and only if:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Its communication boundary satisfies </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\oint</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{v} </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> d\mathbf{A} = </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> (operational closure)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">    \item</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> Its internal semantic field maintains </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> (recursive distinction capacity)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">enumerate</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">theorem</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Closure ensures the system defines its own elements (communications) without external determination. Non-vanishing gradients ensure sufficient distinction density for recursive operations. Capture violates both: platform algorithms breach closure while optimization flattens gradients.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">proof</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Capture as a Phase Transition}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">We define an order parameter:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">m = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\langle</span><span style="color: rgb(215, 186, 125);"> \|</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(215, 186, 125);">\|</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \rangle</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">and a control parameter:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> = \text{optimization pressure},\quad</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">c = \text{semantic complexity capacity}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Mean field dynamics:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\dot{m} = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\gamma</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> - </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">/c) m - </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\kappa</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> m^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">3</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Which yields a supercritical pitchfork bifurcation at </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c = c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Regime | Condition | Interpretation |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">|---|---|---|</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Open cognition | </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &lt; </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | Choices expand, meanings diversify |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Capture onset | </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \approx</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | Society becomes nudgable, brittle |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Semantic death | </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &gt; </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_c</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | Autonomy collapses, gradients vanish |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Turbulent noise | Multi-source </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\beta</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | Competing attractors without coherence |</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This last regime‚Äînot singular authoritarian control‚Äîis the one Ward misclassifies. The 21st century does not produce one dictator of meaning‚Äîit produces many microscopic ones, none stable, all corrosive.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Active Matter Models of Cognition}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The RSVP framework finds striking analogs in active matter physics, where self-propelled particles generate collective flows </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{pisegna2025nonreciprocal}. In particular, the hydrodynamic nonreciprocal Cahn-Hilliard (h-NRCH) model of scalar mixtures coupled to momentum-conserving fluids provides a direct mathematical template.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The h-NRCH equations:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\begin</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\partial</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_t </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> + </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla\cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(\mathbf{u}</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">) </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">= M </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \mu</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> + \text{nonreciprocal terms} </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\rho</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\partial</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_t \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{u}</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\cdot\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \mathbf{u}) </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">&amp;</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">= -</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> p + </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\nabla\cdot</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\boldsymbol{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\sigma</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} + \text{active stress}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\end</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{</span><span style="color: rgb(156, 220, 254);">align</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">map to RSVP via:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| h-NRCH | RSVP |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">|--------|------|</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> (semantic density) |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{u}</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> | </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\mathbf{v}</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> (agency flow) |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Active stress | Platform optimization pressure |</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">| Nonreciprocity | Asymmetric influence between meaning modes |</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This mapping enables importation of stability analysis techniques from active matter, including group velocity calculations and Eckhaus instability thresholds for semantic wave patterns.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Eckhaus Stability Analysis for Semantic Waves}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Consider a traveling semantic wave solution of the form:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t) = A \cos(q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> x - </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\omega</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> t) + </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_m</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">with wave number </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> and frequency </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\omega</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> = v_g q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">To determine the stability of this pattern against long-wavelength modulations, we perform an Eckhaus analysis. Let:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t) = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t) + </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\epsilon</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \delta\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(x,t)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">where </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\epsilon</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);"> \ll</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);"> 1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> and </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> varies slowly in space and time.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The modulation ansatz is:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\delta\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> = \hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}(X,T) e^{i Q x} + \text{c.c.}, \quad X = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\epsilon</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> x, \quad T = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\epsilon</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> t</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">with </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">|Q| </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\ll</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Substituting into the RSVP equations and collecting terms at order </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\epsilon</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">, we obtain the amplitude equation:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\partial</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_T \hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} = </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\xi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\parallel</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(Q) </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\partial</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_X^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} + </span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\xi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\perp</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(Q) \hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} + g|\hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}|^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \hat{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">The Eckhaus stability boundary occurs when the parallel diffusion coefficient vanishes:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\xi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\parallel</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">(Q) = </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">For the RSVP-hNRCH system, this yields:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">Q_E^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> = \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}{</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">3</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\xi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">} \left(</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> - \frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Gamma</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_{\rm P}}{D_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">}\right)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Thus the Eckhaus instability threshold is:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\[</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\boxed{q_E = \sqrt{\frac{</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Gamma</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_{\rm P}}{D_</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\Phi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> \left(</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">1</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> - </span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">3</span><span style="color: rgb(86, 156, 214);">\xi</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> Q^</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">2</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\right)}}}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">\]</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Semantic waves with wave number </span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">q_</span><span style="color: rgb(181, 206, 168);">0</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);"> &lt; q_E</span><span style="color: rgb(78, 201, 176);">$</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);"> are Eckhaus-unstable and will develop sideband modulations leading to pattern collapse.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\section</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Turbulence, Capture, and the Topology of a Cage Without Bars}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">We now enter the core claim: the primary threat is not centralized domination, but decentralized collapse.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Behavioral capture does not converge on a single propagandist or sovereign AI. It converges on many competing attractors, each extracting attention, agency, and meaning, none governing anything in particular </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{bridle2018darkage}.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">This is entropic authoritarianism: power without shape, control without controller, constraint without center </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{prigogine1984order}.</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(220, 220, 170);">\subsection</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{Ward in Phase Space}</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">Ward frames the danger as predictive enclosure---a machine-learned future in which behavior is foreclosed </span><span style="color: rgb(197, 134, 192);">\cite</span><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">{ward2022loop}.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color: rgb(230, 230, 230);">RSVP sharpens this: predictive enclosure occurs only in one quadrant of the field diagram:</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
