<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\chapter{RSVP as a General Theory of Constraint Systems}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\epigraph{``A constraint is a curvature boundary condition in disguise.''}{M. Cabrera (generalized)}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Let any complex adaptive system be modeled by a constraint field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span></span></span></span> and admissible flows <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span> on a state manifold <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">M</span></span></span></span>. RSVP generalizes classical constrained dynamics by rewriting constraint satisfaction as curvature preservation:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo>‚àí</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Œì</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C[\Phi] = \nabla^2 \Phi - \Gamma(\Phi,\nabla\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mopen">[</span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mclose">]</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.9474em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">‚àá</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8641em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">‚àí</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Œì</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord">‚àáŒ¶</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œì</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Œì</span></span></span></span> encodes domain-specific constraint forces.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{theorem}[Constraint‚ÄìCurvature Duality]
\begin{boxed}
A constraint system admits non-degenerate solutions iff the induced curvature tensor of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span></span></span></span> is nonsingular almost everywhere.
\end{boxed}
\end{theorem}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{proof}
Constraint degeneracy occurs when solution classes collapse to equivalence under diffeomorphism, which occurs exactly when <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>det</mi><mo>‚Å°</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\nabla^2 \Phi)=0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0641em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop">det</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">‚àá</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span>, implying singular curvature and loss of admissible deformation modes.
\end{proof}</p>
