<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\chapter{Unification with Computational Mechanics}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\epigraph{``A causal state is a curvature equivalence class.''}{C. Shalizi (formal translation)}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">In computational mechanics, causal equivalence is defined by predictive sufficiency. In RSVP, equivalence classes are induced by identical local curvature:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>‚àº</mo><mi>y</mi><mtext>‚ÄÖ‚Ää</mtext><mo>‚ü∫</mo><mtext>‚ÄÖ‚Ää</mtext><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àÄ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>‚â§</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x \sim y \iff \nabla^k \Phi(x) = \nabla^k \Phi(y) \quad \forall k \le 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚àº</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7194em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">y</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚ü∫</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1491em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">‚àá</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8991em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1491em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">‚àá</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8991em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">y</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mord">‚àÄ</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">‚â§</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">2</span></span></span></span></span>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{definition}[RSVP Causal State]
A causal state is a maximal set of points with identical first and second semantic derivatives.
\end{definition}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{theorem}[Causal‚ÄìCurvature Isomorphism]
\begin{boxed}
The <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>œµ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">œµ</span></span></span></span>-machine causal partition is isomorphic to the curvature partition induced by <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla \Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">‚àáŒ¶</span></span></span></span> and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">‚àá</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Œ¶</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^2 \Phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8141em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">‚àá</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Œ¶</span></span></span></span>.
\end{boxed}
\end{theorem}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{proof}
Predictive equivalence requires identical future-outcome distributions; in differentiable state spaces this holds exactly when local gradient and curvature structures match, since transition kernels depend on first- and second-order state derivatives.
\end{proof}</p>
